---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Picture from '../components/Picture.astro';
import {selected} from '../data/catalog';
import {url} from '../lib/urls';
const path='/about/';
---
<Layout title="About Cubey" description="A personal graphics and simulation workshop, and the engine behind it. The story from early C++ and OpenGL experiments to a new project built with Vulkan and Codex." path={path}>
  <section class="page-title about-hero">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">About Cubey</span>
      <h1>Following the<br/> next idea.</h1>
      <p class="intro">Cubey is my graphics and simulation workshop, and the engine behind it. I build the tools to explore an idea, then let what I learn shape the engine.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href={url('/demos/',path)}>See what I have been making ↗</a>
    </div>
    <figure class="workshop-montage">
      <div><Picture id={selected('ocean-surface').poster} path={path} alt="Ocean waves" eager/></div>
      <div><Picture id={selected('guardian').poster} path={path} alt="The authored Guardian sculpture" eager/></div>
      <figcaption>Ocean and Guardian · two corners of the workshop.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <section class="section about-story">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">Where it started</span><h2>Learning by<br/> building.</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>The first Cubey grew out of learning C++ and OpenGL more than a decade ago. Building a small engine gave me somewhere to try graphics and simulation ideas. Each experiment revealed something else the engine needed, and improving it made the next experiment possible.</p>
      <p>Today's Cubey is a new project in that same spirit. The workshop and the engine have always belonged together: one gives me a reason to build the other.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section about-story">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">Starting again</span><h2>New tools.<br/> Familiar questions.</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>I came back to Cubey with a decade of software engineering behind me, including more than five years in graphics. The field had moved on, and so had my experience. This time the engine uses Vulkan, with a much broader foundation to build on.</p>
      <p>What surprised me was how much still felt worth exploring. Fluid simulation and signed distance fields could hold my attention all over again. More experience brought new questions to those subjects; better tools made room to follow them further.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" id="shared-foundation"><div class="section-head"><h2>The engine grows with the experiments.</h2><p>A useful result can change what becomes possible elsewhere.</p></div><div class="feedback-loop" role="group" aria-label="Experiments and the shared engine support each other"><div><span class="eyebrow">Experiments</span><h3>Try something interesting.</h3><p>A scene puts a technique to work and exposes what it needs.</p></div><div class="feedback-arrows" aria-hidden="true"><span>⇄</span><small>ideas move both ways</small></div><div><span class="eyebrow">Shared engine</span><h3>Build on what proves useful.</h3><p>Rendering, assets, lighting and materials support the next experiment.</p></div></div><div class="grid two connection-notes"><article><h3>Water experiments meet terrain.</h3><p>The 2.5D fluid work fed into the terrain-water experience. Shared terrain rendering carries useful changes between the projects.</p><p class="note">The featured Terrain Water film replays recorded SynxFlow fields. The builtin shallow-water study remains a separate demo.</p><div class="scene-links"><a href={url('/demos/simulation/shallow-water/',path)}>Shallow water ↗</a><a href={url('/demos/terrain/#terrain-water',path)}>Terrain Water ↗</a></div></article><article><h3>A sky becomes a light source.</h3><p>Atmosphere and clouds became more than a standalone view. Their shared lighting and backdrop also support the material workshops.</p><div class="scene-links"><a href={url('/demos/atmosphere/',path)}>Atmosphere &amp; light ↗</a><a href={url('/demos/materials/',path)}>Material workshops ↗</a></div></article></div></section>
  <section class="section about-story">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">How I work</span><h2>Exploring further<br/> with Codex.</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>Working with Codex makes a larger workshop practical. As a coding collaborator, it helps me build more of the foundation, investigate more approaches and spend longer refining each result. I choose the questions and direction, and review what we build.</p>
      <p>The subjects still follow my interests. A familiar fluid experiment can branch into several solvers; a terrain idea can turn into a long investigation. Connections often emerge along the way.</p>
      <p>I want to understand established techniques well enough to put them to work. That takes comparisons, checks and revisions. Sometimes an approach needs rebuilding; sometimes I leave it behind. What I follow next is still open.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href={url('/demos/',path)}>Explore the demos ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section about-story"><h2>Look closer.</h2><div class="body-copy"><p>The source, build guides and technical notes live alongside the experiments. The original OpenGL project is preserved on the legacy branch.</p><div class="scene-links"><a href="https://github.com/byebyebryan/cubey">Source repository ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/byebyebryan/cubey/tree/legacy">Original Cubey ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/byebyebryan/cubey/blob/main/docs/getting-started.md">Build and run ↗</a><a href={url('/credits/',path)}>Media and credits ↗</a></div></div></section>
</Layout>
